windows.media.playback.mediaplayer: MediaSource, AdaptiveMediaSource, MediaPlaybackItem and a frame-server MediaPlayer;
mfmediaengine: TransferVideoFrame to a WIC bitmap reads bottom-up frames upright.

Lightroom Classic's Help > What's New showed "Unable to play this video. It requires a component that isn't available
for this computer" for its four videos (local H.264 files in %TEMP%, LrCHelp_*.mp4). video_toolkit.dll first activates
Windows.Media.Core.MediaSource (IMediaSourceStatics) and Windows.Media.Streaming.Adaptive.AdaptiveMediaSource
(IAdaptiveMediaSourceStatics, which it calls without checking that the factory exists, so it crashed once
MediaSource was there). For a local file it then uses MediaSource.CreateFromUri and IMediaSource4, and plays the video
with its own IMFMediaEngine in frame-server mode; it does not create a MediaPlayer.

New in the DLL (media_private.idl: interface layouts from the Windows metadata, with IInspectable for types Wine does
not declare):
- MediaSource: CreateFromUri (keeps the absolute URI), IMediaSource2/3/4 (OpenAsync completes at once; the media engine
  opens the file when it plays), IMediaPlaybackSource, IClosable.
- AdaptiveMediaSource statics: IsContentTypeSupported answers FALSE; the async creators return E_NOTIMPL, so callers
  fall back to MediaSource (HLS and DASH manifests are not supported).
- MediaPlaybackItem (IMediaPlaybackItemFactory.Create).
- MediaPlayer on IMFMediaEngine: Source (IMediaPlayerSource2) and SetUriSource, Play/Pause, AutoPlay, looping, mute,
  volume, rate, position, duration; IMediaPlayer3's PlaybackSession (state, natural size and duration, position) and
  events (MediaOpened/Ended/Failed, CurrentStateChanged, SourceChanged, PlaybackStateChanged...) raised from a worker
  thread outside the engine's callbacks; IMediaPlayer5's frame server: VideoFrameAvailable while playing, and
  CopyFrameToVideoSurface(WithTargetRectangle) takes the frame at native size into a WIC bitmap and scales it into the
  target IDirect3DSurface's texture (via IDirect3DDxgiInterfaceAccess; BGRA/BGRX/RGBA).

mfmediaengine: media_engine_transfer_wic copied the converter's output top-down, but its RGB32 samples are bottom-up
(negative MF_MT_DEFAULT_STRIDE), so frames arrived upside down. It now reads rows as the window path of patch 0144
does: a 2D buffer by its own pitch, a plain buffer by the sink type's default stride (bottom-up when it has none).

Test: tests/mf/mfwinrt.c (MediaSource.CreateFromUri -> MediaPlaybackItem -> MediaPlayer frame server ->
CopyFrameToVideoSurface into a 427x240 texture from mkvideo.sh's 854x480 layout.mp4): PASS, white top, red left, blue
right (before: no MediaSource class; with the player but without the mfmediaengine part, upside down). Lightroom Classic
(November 2026) What's New: the Glow and Blemish Removal videos play (before: the error above).

